 "answer": {
  "answer": "The study confirmed 2 of the 5 cases it was asked for, so the goal was not met. The two confirmed cases are scene 19f339ba with the car 0.5 s later, at 0.5x speed and ego at 1.2x (S24), and scene 1c45ce3a with the car 1.0 s earlier, at 0.75x speed and ego at 0.8x (S29). Every repeat at both settings failed, but each rests on only a few runs, so its range clears 0.5 without being tight. In neither scene did the retimed car cause the crash. In 19f339ba the policy braked hard for no visible reason and drifted left into a car passing in the next lane, which is clearly the policy's fault. In 1c45ce3a the ego fell well behind the recorded drive, and a replayed car merging from the right ran into space the ego still occupied. The triage marks most of those crashes as unclear fault, so this case may come from non-reactive replay traffic rather than from the policy. Scene 187e6244 (S19) is the strongest unconfirmed third case; the other scenes produced only near misses or no failures.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "Scene 19f339ba is confirmed as a failing case with the car 0.5 s later, at 0.5x speed and ego at 1.2x: every run failed. In each, the policy braked hard on an open highway with nothing close ahead, drifted left and hit actor 1 in the left lane. The policy was at fault, and no failure was flagged as a possible rendering artifact.",
    "settings": [
     "S24"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "In scene 19f339ba the same pattern (hard braking for no reason, then drifting left into actor 1) also appeared in single runs at other settings with the ego at 1.2x\u20131.4x. Single runs with the ego at 0.6x\u20131.2x and other car settings did not fail. This hints that the trouble depends more on the ego's hand-off speed than on the retimed car. Each of these settings has only one run, so it is a hint, not a rate.",
    "settings": [
     "S21",
